1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,339
Hi, I'm Srijan, and this is Incident-Zero, our Object-Oriented Programming project from
Group 76.

2
00:00:06,339 --> 00:00:09,862
It is a help desk for campus IT at BITS
Pilani Hyderabad.

3
00:00:09,862 --> 00:00:15,430
A student reports a problem, the system sorts
it and assigns it, and a technician fixes it.

4
00:00:15,430 --> 00:00:20,682
Every step is checked by a state machine and
written to a log that cannot be edited.

5
00:00:20,682 --> 00:00:25,357
And yes, it looks like an arcade game. That
part was just for fun.

6
00:00:26,607 --> 00:00:33,089
In this video I will walk through the repository, open the important files, and
explain what each one does.

7
00:00:33,089 --> 00:00:37,075
Then I will run the app and connect every
click back to the code.

8
00:00:38,325 --> 00:00:42,725
This is the repository on GitHub. The readme
gives the short version.

9
00:00:42,725 --> 00:00:46,510
One Java backend, one React app, and one
PostgreSQL database.

10
00:00:46,510 --> 00:00:53,516
A ticket is reported, classified, prioritised, assigned, investigated,
resolved, verified, and finally closed.

11
00:00:53,516 --> 00:00:59,054
Let me go through the folders. The backend folder holds the Java code, built with Spring
Boot.

12
00:00:59,054 --> 00:01:03,093
The frontend folder holds the React app,
written in TypeScript.

13
00:01:03,093 --> 00:01:09,076
Inside docs there are fifteen decision records. Each one explains why we made a
design choice.

14
00:01:09,076 --> 00:01:13,907
The scripts folder has our verification
scripts, which I will run at the end.

15
00:01:13,907 --> 00:01:17,924
Deploy has the Docker Compose file for the
database and the app.

16
00:01:17,924 --> 00:01:22,143
And the workflows folder runs our checks
automatically on every push.

17
00:01:22,143 --> 00:01:25,217
The other folders are tooling, so I will skip
them.

18
00:01:25,217 --> 00:01:28,429
The real work is in the backend, so let's
start there.

19
00:01:29,679 --> 00:01:35,275
Under the main package, the code is split into modules, one folder for each
responsibility.

20
00:01:35,275 --> 00:01:39,420
Incident is the biggest one. It owns the
ticket and its life cycle.

21
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:45,162
Assignment picks a technician, SLA handles deadlines, and audit keeps the tamper-evident
log.

22
00:01:45,162 --> 00:01:52,121
Identity handles login, notification pushes live updates, and intelligence holds the
suggestion features.

23
00:01:52,121 --> 00:01:57,926
Catalog, knowledge and shared hold the calendar, the help articles, and common
building blocks.

24
00:01:57,926 --> 00:02:01,024
Open the incident module and you see four
folders.

25
00:02:01,024 --> 00:02:06,844
Domain is plain Java. It holds the business rules and knows nothing about Spring or the
database.

26
00:02:06,844 --> 00:02:09,663
Application runs the use cases, step by step.

27
00:02:09,663 --> 00:02:15,429
Infrastructure talks to the outside world, and api is what other modules are allowed to
see.

28
00:02:15,429 --> 00:02:20,901
The rule is simple. Outer layers can depend on inner ones, but never the other way
around.

29
00:02:20,901 --> 00:02:24,710
So how do we make sure nobody breaks that
rule?

30
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:31,777
With a test. This rule says no class in a domain package may depend on Spring, JPA, or
Jackson.

31
00:02:31,777 --> 00:02:34,850
If someone adds one such import, the build
fails.

32
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:40,477
Now the domain folder of the incident module.
This is the part I built.

33
00:02:40,477 --> 00:02:46,442
Notice the file names: NewState, AssignedState, ResolvedState, and so on. I
will come back to those.

34
00:02:46,442 --> 00:02:48,450
First, the main class, Incident.

35
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:53,124
This factory method is the only way to create
a ticket.

36
00:02:53,124 --> 00:02:58,199
The title must be between five and two
hundred characters, or it throws an error.

37
00:02:58,199 --> 00:03:04,010
The description needs at least ten characters, and impact and urgency must be
one, two, or three.

38
00:03:04,010 --> 00:03:09,859
Only when everything is valid does it build the object and record an IncidentCreated
event.

39
00:03:09,859 --> 00:03:16,746
So a broken ticket can never exist. The rules live inside the object, and that is
encapsulation.

40
00:03:17,996 --> 00:03:22,512
These are the actions: acknowledge, resolve,
verify, close, and the rest.

41
00:03:22,512 --> 00:03:27,681
Look how short they are. Each one just asks
the current state object to do the work.

42
00:03:27,681 --> 00:03:31,052
The Incident itself never checks which state
it is in.

43
00:03:31,052 --> 00:03:36,833
Whatever the state returns goes into commit. It switches to the next state and stores the
events.

44
00:03:36,833 --> 00:03:42,658
It also raises the version number, which helps us catch two people changing the same
ticket.

45
00:03:42,658 --> 00:03:47,033
Later, the application layer pulls those
events out and publishes them.

46
00:03:48,283 --> 00:03:52,994
So where do the rules live? In this
interface, IncidentState.

47
00:03:52,994 --> 00:03:58,974
It is sealed, and it permits exactly eleven state classes. Nobody can add a twelfth by
accident.

48
00:03:58,974 --> 00:04:04,621
Every action has a default body, and that default simply throws an illegal transition
error.

49
00:04:04,621 --> 00:04:10,280
So by default, nothing is allowed. A state has to override a method to make that move
legal.

50
00:04:11,530 --> 00:04:14,715
Here is AssignedState. It overrides
acknowledge.

51
00:04:14,715 --> 00:04:18,515
First it checks that the caller is the
assigned technician.

52
00:04:18,515 --> 00:04:20,571
Then it stops the response clock.

53
00:04:20,571 --> 00:04:24,855
And it returns the next state, Acknowledged,
together with an event.

54
00:04:24,855 --> 00:04:32,182
The same call behaves differently depending on the state object. That is polymorphism,
and this is the State pattern.

55
00:04:32,182 --> 00:04:34,862
It replaces a long chain of if-else checks.

56
00:04:36,112 --> 00:04:41,855
One more example, ResolvedState. Only the person who reported the ticket can verify the
fix.

57
00:04:41,855 --> 00:04:44,551
And the rating must be between one and five.

58
00:04:44,551 --> 00:04:47,973
If they reject the fix instead, the ticket is
reopened.

59
00:04:49,223 --> 00:04:56,230
An illegal move throws this exception. It sits with fourteen other error classes, all
extending one base class.

60
00:04:56,230 --> 00:05:02,233
Each error carries its own code and its own HTTP status. That is inheritance doing useful
work.

61
00:05:03,483 --> 00:05:08,321
Next, time. Every ticket has two deadlines:
one to respond, and one to resolve.

62
00:05:08,321 --> 00:05:12,627
Each deadline is an SlaClock, and it is a
record, so it never changes.

63
00:05:12,627 --> 00:05:17,672
Pause does not modify the clock. It returns a
new clock with the time used so far.

64
00:05:17,672 --> 00:05:21,175
And it asks a calendar how much working time
has passed.

65
00:05:22,425 --> 00:05:29,209
Back in Incident, this method reads both clocks. At seventy-five percent used, the
ticket is marked at risk.

66
00:05:29,209 --> 00:05:34,558
Past the target, it is breached, and a breach
is permanent. It never goes back to on track.

67
00:05:35,808 --> 00:05:41,266
That calendar is this class. A working day is
a day of the work week that is not a holiday.

68
00:05:41,266 --> 00:05:45,383
It walks day by day and adds only the hours
inside the working window.

69
00:05:45,383 --> 00:05:50,275
So a low priority ticket raised on Friday
evening does not breach over the weekend.

70
00:05:51,525 --> 00:05:55,842
One more trick. The code never asks the
computer for the time directly.

71
00:05:55,842 --> 00:05:58,450
It asks this small interface, ClockPort.

72
00:05:58,450 --> 00:06:03,841
The real implementation is a virtual clock.
It multiplies elapsed time by a scale factor.

73
00:06:03,841 --> 00:06:09,082
So in a demo, one real second can count as
thirty, and I can watch a deadline approach.

74
00:06:09,082 --> 00:06:14,100
That is abstraction. The domain depends on an
interface, not on the system clock.

75
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:21,377
Now the application layer. When a ticket comes in, it goes through this folder, the
intake pipeline.

76
00:06:21,377 --> 00:06:25,859
The pipeline receives every step and sorts
them by their order number.

77
00:06:25,859 --> 00:06:30,495
Then it runs them one after another, passing
the same context object along.

78
00:06:31,745 --> 00:06:38,309
There are nine steps. Validate checks the input. Sanitize strips HTML and hides emails
and phone numbers.

79
00:06:38,309 --> 00:06:43,739
Then we create the incident, link it to a master ticket if there is one, and pick a
category.

80
00:06:43,739 --> 00:06:49,518
Enrich looks for exam words, then come priority, assignment, and finally saving to
the database.

81
00:06:50,768 --> 00:06:54,892
All nine extend this abstract class. Its
execute method is final.

82
00:06:54,892 --> 00:06:58,894
It handles timing and error logging the same
way for every step.

83
00:06:58,894 --> 00:07:02,151
Each step only fills in this one method,
doExecute.

84
00:07:02,151 --> 00:07:08,106
The parent fixes the skeleton and the children fill the gap. That is the Template
Method pattern.

85
00:07:09,356 --> 00:07:15,074
For example, step one refuses a title under
five characters before anything else happens.

86
00:07:16,324 --> 00:07:21,999
Step seven is the interesting one. It asks a
priority strategy to calculate the priority.

87
00:07:21,999 --> 00:07:26,221
Then it looks up the SLA policy for that
service tier and priority.

88
00:07:26,221 --> 00:07:30,914
And it calls classify on the incident, which
moves it from New to Classified.

89
00:07:32,164 --> 00:07:36,405
The default strategy is a simple matrix of
impact against urgency.

90
00:07:36,405 --> 00:07:41,427
After that come the bumps. During an exam
window, the priority goes up one level.

91
00:07:41,427 --> 00:07:46,146
There are three strategies behind one
interface. That is the Strategy pattern.

92
00:07:47,396 --> 00:07:53,100
After intake, every action on a ticket goes
through this class, IncidentCommandService.

93
00:07:53,100 --> 00:07:56,282
Every method has the same shape. Load the
incident.

94
00:07:56,282 --> 00:07:58,276
Check that this user is allowed.

95
00:07:58,276 --> 00:08:00,479
Call the domain method we just saw.

96
00:08:00,479 --> 00:08:04,508
Then save it and publish its events, all
inside one transaction.

97
00:08:04,508 --> 00:08:06,785
If anything fails, nothing is saved.

98
00:08:08,035 --> 00:08:12,160
And who is allowed? That is decided here, in
one switch.

99
00:08:12,160 --> 00:08:15,541
Only the assigned technician can acknowledge
or resolve.

100
00:08:15,541 --> 00:08:19,417
Only the student who reported it can verify
or reject the fix.

101
00:08:19,417 --> 00:08:23,235
And only a team lead or an admin can declare
a major incident.

102
00:08:24,485 --> 00:08:30,238
Choosing the technician works the same way as
priority. One interface, AssignmentStrategy.

103
00:08:30,238 --> 00:08:36,183
And seven implementations: round robin, least loaded, skill match, experience, and a few
more.

104
00:08:36,183 --> 00:08:40,277
There is even one that assigns a whole batch
of tickets together.

105
00:08:40,277 --> 00:08:44,512
Least loaded first removes anyone who is off
shift or already full.

106
00:08:44,512 --> 00:08:47,870
Then it sorts by load and takes the least
busy person.

107
00:08:47,870 --> 00:08:53,230
Because they share one interface, changing
the strategy is a setting, not a code change.

108
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:59,282
Now the outer layer, infrastructure. This is
where the outside world connects.

109
00:08:59,282 --> 00:09:04,013
IncidentController is the REST entry point.
This method handles a new ticket.

110
00:09:04,013 --> 00:09:09,340
It reads the request, hands it to the intake
service, and returns the created ticket.

111
00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:13,773
Notice there are no business rules here. The
controller only translates.

112
00:09:13,773 --> 00:09:17,128
The second method takes the action name from
the URL.

113
00:09:17,128 --> 00:09:20,615
A switch maps each name to the matching
service method.

114
00:09:21,865 --> 00:09:28,141
For the database we use the same idea. The domain defines an interface called
IncidentRepository.

115
00:09:28,141 --> 00:09:30,504
This adapter implements it with JPA.

116
00:09:30,504 --> 00:09:36,646
It converts the incident to a snapshot and back, so the domain class has no database
annotations.

117
00:09:36,646 --> 00:09:41,680
This is the Adapter pattern. We could change
the database without touching the domain.

118
00:09:42,930 --> 00:09:45,437
Now my favourite part, the audit ledger.

119
00:09:45,437 --> 00:09:50,942
This listener hears every event, and it runs
inside the same transaction as the change.

120
00:09:50,942 --> 00:09:54,709
It locks the incident row and reads the hash
of the last entry.

121
00:09:54,709 --> 00:09:58,503
Then it computes a new hash that includes
that previous hash.

122
00:09:58,503 --> 00:10:05,361
The hasher joins the previous hash, the time, the actor, the action and the payload, then
runs SHA-256.

123
00:10:05,361 --> 00:10:11,413
So every entry is chained to the one before. Change one old row, and every later hash
stops matching.

124
00:10:11,413 --> 00:10:15,178
But what stops someone from quietly editing
the table itself?

125
00:10:15,178 --> 00:10:20,311
The database itself refuses to update or
delete audit rows. This trigger blocks it.

126
00:10:21,561 --> 00:10:26,949
Security is configured in one place. Login is
open, and everything else needs a token.

127
00:10:26,949 --> 00:10:30,073
And the admin routes need the administrator
role.

128
00:10:31,323 --> 00:10:35,153
Finally, errors. This handler catches any of
our exceptions.

129
00:10:35,153 --> 00:10:39,498
It turns them into a standard error response
with our own error code.

130
00:10:39,498 --> 00:10:43,257
So the frontend always gets a clear reason,
never a stack trace.

131
00:10:44,507 --> 00:10:47,994
The frontend is much simpler. Let me go back
and open it.

132
00:10:47,994 --> 00:10:53,728
Services talk to the backend, context holds login and live updates, and pages are the
screens.

133
00:10:54,978 --> 00:10:59,687
Every request goes through this one function.
It attaches the login token.

134
00:10:59,687 --> 00:11:04,183
If the server sends an error, it throws it
with the server's own message.

135
00:11:05,433 --> 00:11:08,838
Switching user logs in again and stores the
new token.

136
00:11:10,088 --> 00:11:16,479
This is the submit handler of the report form. It calls create with the title,
description and service.

137
00:11:17,729 --> 00:11:23,244
And this is the technician screen. A button is drawn only if its action is in the allowed
list.

138
00:11:23,244 --> 00:11:29,229
That list comes from the state object in the backend. The frontend never decides what is
legal.

139
00:11:29,229 --> 00:11:32,151
Okay, enough code. Let's actually run it.

140
00:11:33,401 --> 00:11:35,504
I am logged in as Aarav, a student.

141
00:11:35,504 --> 00:11:38,433
His Moodle quiz will not open, so he reports
it.

142
00:11:38,433 --> 00:11:43,690
As I type, the copilot reads the text and
suggests the affected service, Moodle LMS.

143
00:11:43,690 --> 00:11:46,009
I set the urgency to high, and submit.

144
00:11:47,259 --> 00:11:50,718
Pause. This click runs the submit handler I
showed you.

145
00:11:50,718 --> 00:11:53,647
It sends a POST request to the incidents
route.

146
00:11:53,647 --> 00:11:57,005
The controller receives it and calls the
intake service.

147
00:11:57,005 --> 00:11:59,068
The pipeline runs its nine steps.

148
00:11:59,068 --> 00:12:04,252
Step three calls the factory method, which
checks the rules and records the event.

149
00:12:04,252 --> 00:12:07,559
Step nine saves the ticket and publishes its
events.

150
00:12:07,559 --> 00:12:11,545
And the audit listener writes the first links
of the hash chain.

151
00:12:12,795 --> 00:12:18,063
And here is the result. The ticket is already
assigned to a technician.

152
00:12:18,063 --> 00:12:23,231
It came out as priority two. The word quiz
triggered the exam bump we saw in the matrix.

153
00:12:23,231 --> 00:12:29,406
For this service and priority, the policy gives fifteen minutes to respond and two
hours to resolve.

154
00:12:29,406 --> 00:12:33,478
Below is the timeline, with one hashed entry
for each step so far.

155
00:12:34,728 --> 00:12:37,813
Now I switch to the technician it was
assigned to.

156
00:12:37,813 --> 00:12:43,381
The ticket is in his queue. There is no Resolve button yet, because the state does
not allow it.

157
00:12:43,381 --> 00:12:45,066
He clicks acknowledge.

158
00:12:46,316 --> 00:12:49,859
The button calls runCommand, which posts the
action name.

159
00:12:49,859 --> 00:12:53,352
The controller's switch picks the acknowledge
method.

160
00:12:53,352 --> 00:12:56,743
The service loads the ticket and asks the
authorizer.

161
00:12:56,743 --> 00:12:58,741
He is the assignee, so it passes.

162
00:12:58,741 --> 00:13:02,760
AssignedState stops the response clock and
returns the next state.

163
00:13:04,010 --> 00:13:08,202
The state is now Acknowledged, and the
response clock has stopped.

164
00:13:08,202 --> 00:13:12,224
He starts the investigation, and the state
becomes In Progress.

165
00:13:12,224 --> 00:13:15,742
He needs more details, so he asks the student
a question.

166
00:13:16,992 --> 00:13:20,761
In the code, InProgressState pauses the
resolution clock.

167
00:13:20,761 --> 00:13:24,777
That is the pause method from earlier,
returning a paused clock.

168
00:13:26,027 --> 00:13:30,630
So while we wait for the student, the
technician is not blamed for the delay.

169
00:13:30,630 --> 00:13:33,767
Back to Aarav. He sees the question and
answers it.

170
00:13:33,767 --> 00:13:37,263
The state goes back to In Progress, and the
clock runs again.

171
00:13:38,513 --> 00:13:43,120
The technician fixes the problem and resolves
the ticket with a proper note.

172
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:49,946
The note needs at least twenty characters. A lazy note like "done" is rejected by the
domain.

173
00:13:51,196 --> 00:13:54,992
Aarav checks Moodle, gives five stars, and
confirms the fix.

174
00:13:54,992 --> 00:13:57,889
This sends the verify action with the rating.

175
00:13:57,889 --> 00:14:02,972
ResolvedState checks that he is the reporter,
then moves the ticket to Verified.

176
00:14:04,222 --> 00:14:06,081
The ticket is now Verified.

177
00:14:06,081 --> 00:14:12,980
Finally the technician closes it. The ticket is closed, and the state allows no further
moves.

178
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:18,216
And the timeline shows the full story: who
did what, when, and the hash of each entry.

179
00:14:19,466 --> 00:14:24,009
Now let me try to cheat. I will ask the
server to close that ticket again.

180
00:14:24,009 --> 00:14:30,009
The server says no. Error 409, with the
current state and the command I tried.

181
00:14:30,009 --> 00:14:33,539
That is the default method in IncidentState,
doing its job.

182
00:14:34,789 --> 00:14:39,498
Next, the audit ledger. As the admin, I ask
the server to verify every chain.

183
00:14:39,498 --> 00:14:43,138
It recomputes every hash and compares it with
the stored one.

184
00:14:43,138 --> 00:14:44,851
Every entry is valid.

185
00:14:46,101 --> 00:14:49,635
Now I try to change an old entry directly in
the database.

186
00:14:49,635 --> 00:14:53,657
The database refuses. The audit table is
append only.

187
00:14:54,907 --> 00:15:01,057
Last, the tests. This script fires fifty thousand random command sequences at the
state machine.

188
00:15:01,057 --> 00:15:07,533
Twenty-four checks pass, covering more than five hundred and fifty thousand transitions,
in a few seconds.

189
00:15:07,533 --> 00:15:11,392
And the architecture rules I showed at the
start all pass too.

190
00:15:12,642 --> 00:15:20,183
That is Incident-Zero. It was built by Group 76: Suhani Agarwal, Kartikey Negi, Harish
Kumar, and me, Srijan Shukla.

191
00:15:21,433 --> 00:15:30,020
The object-oriented ideas were not decoration. Encapsulation, inheritance,
polymorphism and abstraction each solved a real problem here.

192
00:15:30,020 --> 00:15:34,449
The code is on GitHub, at the address on
screen. Thank you for watching.
